)
```

## AI Usage Guidelines

### 1. When to Use Domain-Specific Type Aliases
Use domain-specific aliases when you need types from a specific domain:

```go
// ✅ When working with facts-specific types
import (
    spookytypesfacts "spooky/internal/types/facts"
)

func ProcessFacts(collection *spookytypesfacts.FactCollection) error {
    // Use facts-specific types
    return nil
}
```

### 2. When to Use Unified Types Alias
Use the unified types alias for general type access:

```go
// ✅ When working with multiple type domains
import (
    spookytypes "spooky/internal/types"
)

func ProcessData(config *spookytypes.Config, facts *spookytypes.FactCollection) error {
    // Use unified types access
    return nil
}
```

### 3. When to Use Internal Package Aliases
Use internal package aliases for package functionality:

```go
// ✅ When using package functionality
import (
    spookyinterfaces "spooky/internal/interfaces"
    spookylogging "spooky/internal/logging"
)

func NewManager() spookyinterfaces.FactManager {
    logger := spookylogging.GetLogger()
    // ...
}
```

### 4. When to Use Subpackage Aliases
Use subpackage aliases for specific functionality:

```go
// ✅ When using specific subpackage functionality
import (
    spookyfactscollectorsssh "spooky/internal/facts/collectors/ssh"
    spookysshclient "spooky/internal/ssh/client"
)

func NewSSHCollector() *spookyfactscollectorsssh.Collector {
    client := spookysshclient.NewClient()
    // ...
}
```

## Import Organization

### Standard Import Order
Organize imports in the following order:

```go
import (
    // 1. Standard library imports
    "fmt"
    "io"
    "time"

    // 2. Third-party imports
    "github.com/some/package"

    // 3. Internal package imports (alphabetical)
    spookyactions "spooky/internal/actions"
    spookyconfig "spooky/internal/config"
    spookyinterfaces "spooky/internal/interfaces"
    spookylogging "spooky/internal/logging"

    // 4. Type imports (alphabetical)
    spookytypes "spooky/internal/types"
    spookytypesfacts "spooky/internal/types/facts"
    spookytypesmachines "spooky/internal/types/machines"
)
```

### Grouping Guidelines
- Group imports by type (stdlib, third-party, internal, types)
- Sort alphabetically within each group
- Use blank lines to separate groups
- Keep related imports together

